    "lastAttempt": null,
    "locked": false,
    "marks": 3,
    "modelAnswer": "Trifluoroethanoic acid is the stronger acid. This is because fluorine is highly electronegative and withdraws electron density from the carboxyl group through the inductive effect. With three fluorine atoms present in trifluoroethanoic acid compared to two in difluoroethanoic acid, the electron-withdrawing effect is more pronounced, stabilizing the carboxylate anion ($CF_3COO^-$) more effectively. This greater stabilization of the conjugate base makes trifluoroethanoic acid more readily ionize, resulting in a lower $pK_a$ value and therefore greater acid strength.",
